{
  "threads": [
    {
      "index": 1,
      "rootCommentId": 4344406429,
      "threadId": "PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6vCqMa",
      "path": ".github/workflows/ci.yml",
      "line": 899,
      "resolved": false,
      "bot": false,
      "body": "⚠️ **IMPORTANT (please confirm) — does `continue-on-error: true` also cover the cache *post-save* phase?**\n\nThe comment above says a dead cache \"keeps [from] reding the shard\", but this step-level flag is verified here only for the **restore** path (which is what stalled on the v1.0.621 run). An `actions/cache` step also runs a runner-injected `Post:` save step, which per the PR's own Notes cannot carry a step-level `timeout-minutes` — and it is unclear whether a step-level `continue-on-error` masks a *failing* post-save on a degraded cache service the same way it masks a failing restore.\n\nIf it does not, then a degraded service failing at **save** time (the same event class this PR guards against) would still red the shard after all tests are green — exactly the gh-1724 fail-after-green shape (`ci_core_shard_upload_retry_guard_test.dart` documents that precedent, and that guard deliberately keeps its retries non-`continue-on-error` for loud failure).\n\nPlease confirm against the GitHub Actions runner/docs behavior for `continue-on-error` × post steps:\n\n- If post steps **are** covered: no code change needed — just tighten the comment/CHANGELOG wording to say the best-effort contract covers restore and post-save.\n- If post steps are **not** covered: the residual is bigger than the Notes state (\"only the post-save *timeout* is uncovered\"); the 20-min job budget backstops a save hang but not a fast save failure.\n\nEither way this does not change the restore-path fix, which is correct and matches the measured evidence."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "rootCommentId": 4344406598,
      "threadId": "PRRT_kwDOTXdlLc6vCqOM",
      "path": "test/ci_core_shard_timeout_guard_test.dart",
      "line": 159,
      "resolved": false,
      "bot": false,
      "body": "💡 **SUGGESTION: inconsistent `name` fallback in the AC3 failure reason**\n\nThe other two expects in this file use `step['name'] ?? step['uses']` (lines 130 and 145) so the reason still names the step when `name:` is absent, but this one interpolates `step['name']` bare — an unnamed cache step would render `\"cache step \"null\" stalls are capped at 2 min …\"`.\n\n```dart\n'cache step \"${step['name'] ?? step['uses']}\" stalls are capped at '\n```\n\nCosmetic (both current steps carry names, so today's messages are fine), but it keeps the failure reasons uniform if a third cache step is ever added and tripped by this guard."
    }
  ],
  "census": {
    "source": "graphql",
    "graphqlTotal": 2,
    "graphqlUnresolved": 2
  }
}

